Install Jenkins in one-server
Install Splunk in one-server


Access it: http://<SPLUNK_PUBLIC_IP>:8000 


S1: Enable HTTP Event Collector (HEC)
Go to:

Settings
→ Data Inputs
→ HTTP Event Collector
Click Global Settings.

Enable:

✔ All Tokens
Save.


S2: Create an HEC Token
Go to:

Settings
→ Data Inputs
→ HTTP Event Collector
→ New Token

Configure:

Field	Value
Name	JenkinsHEC
Source name	Jenkins
Source type	jenkins
Index	main

Click Next until Submit.


S3: Test HEC from the Jenkins EC2

From your Jenkins server, run:

curl -k https://<SPLUNK_IP>:8088/services/collector \
-H "Authorization: Splunk YOUR_HEC_TOKEN" \
-H "Content-Type: application/json" \
-d '{"event":"Hello from Jenkins"}'

Expected response:

{
  "text":"Success",
  "code":0
}


S4: Verify the Event in Splunk
In Splunk Search & Reporting, search:
source=Jenkins


S5: Store the Token in Jenkins
Go to:
Manage Jenkins
→ Credentials
→ System
→ Global credentials
Add a Secret text credential:
Kind: Secret text
Secret: Your HEC Token
ID: splunk-hec-token
Save.


S6: Test it from pipeline
pipeline {
    agent any

    environment {
        SPLUNK_URL = 'https://<SPLUNK_IP>:8088/services/collector'
    }

    stages {
        stage('Build') {
            steps {
                echo 'Building...'
            }
        }

        stage('Send Event to Splunk') {
            steps {
                withCredentials([string(credentialsId: 'splunk-hec-token', variable: 'HEC_TOKEN')]) {
                    sh '''
                    curl -k $SPLUNK_URL \
                    -H "Authorization: Splunk $HEC_TOKEN" \
                    -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
                    -d '{
                        "event":{
                            "job":"'"$JOB_NAME"'",
                            "build":"'"$BUILD_NUMBER"'",
                            "status":"SUCCESS"
                        }
                    }'
                    '''
                }
            }
        }
    }
}


Splunk Vs Jenkins:

Jenkins alone is enough when:
You have one Jenkins server.
You only need to know whether a build passed or failed.
You want to view console logs for a specific build.
Your team is small and pipelines are straightforward.

For example:

Build #120
✓ SUCCESS
Duration: 4m 12s
Triggered by: GitHub Webhook

Jenkins displays this very well.

Where Splunk becomes valuable

Imagine your company has:

15 Jenkins controllers
300 pipelines
50 developers
Kubernetes
AWS CloudWatch
Linux servers
Nginx
Application logs
Security logs

Now management asks:

"Why did deployments fail yesterday between 2 PM and 3 PM?"

Jenkins alone cannot easily correlate all that information.

Splunk can search across all those data sources in one place.

Example 1: Enterprise Dashboard

A DevOps lead wants to see:

Today's Builds
---------------
Success : 920
Failed  : 35
Running : 12

Instead of opening many Jenkins jobs, Splunk can display this in a dashboard.

Example 2: Root Cause Analysis

Suppose a deployment failed.

Splunk can correlate:

Jenkins build event
Kubernetes pod logs
Nginx logs
Application logs
Linux system logs

Timeline:

10:05 Jenkins deployment started
10:06 Kubernetes Pod CrashLoopBackOff
10:06 Application OutOfMemoryError
10:07 Nginx 502 Bad Gateway

Without Splunk, you'd need to log into multiple systems separately.

Example 3: SLA Reporting

Your manager asks:

"How many deployments succeeded this month?"

In Splunk:

index=jenkins status=SUCCESS

You can build reports and charts for management.

Example 4: Security

Splunk can answer:

Who triggered deployments?
Which user deployed to production?
Which IP address was used?
Which Jenkins job executed?

This is useful for auditing and compliance.

Example 5: Alerts

Suppose five builds fail in a row.

Splunk can automatically:

Send an email
Send a Slack notification
Create a ServiceNow incident
Open a Jira ticket

before someone even checks Jenkins.

Example 6: Multiple Jenkins Servers

Many enterprises have separate Jenkins environments:

Jenkins DEV
Jenkins QA
Jenkins UAT
Jenkins PROD

Splunk collects events from all of them into a single searchable platform.


| Feature                            | Jenkins | Splunk            |
| ---------------------------------- | ------- | ----------------- |
| Build history                      | ✅       | ✅ (if integrated) |
| Console logs                       | ✅       | ✅                 |
| Search across many systems         | ❌       | ✅                 |
| Correlate logs from multiple tools | ❌       | ✅                 |
| Long-term analytics                | Limited | ✅                 |
| Executive dashboards               | Limited | ✅                 |
| Alerting based on patterns         | Basic   | ✅                 |
| Security and audit analysis        | Limited | ✅                 |
